so you're involved in this united city  campaign in manchester  what is it trying to achieve i think  it's trying to take control of the  messaging locally  um i think that eight months after this  virus starting we first went into  lockdown  to not have a clear plan out of this is  something that we're finding difficult  to understand why and we're frustrated  and  i think that obviously we've been  putting the toughest tears which is  something that  again brings greater  problems for us i mean we've got  economic problems got health problems  uh mental health issues there's a lack  of um  understanding of how we're going to come  out of it uncertainty  and we just felt the business community  in manchester has always been resilient  manning unions have always come together  to come out of crises like this before  and it's about time now that we started  to think about how the future looks  short medium and long term you have to  be part of a lot of groups a lot of  campaigns but this one it just felt like  there was a group of  about 120 of us now who just felt that  we weren't getting the messaging that we  wanted from  from government and you of course run  hotels in manchester so your businessman  there  how's it been it must be been really  hard it's shocking  it's shocking to be i have to say the  whole hospitality sector has been  devastated and i split it probably into  two parts  with the restrictions in place we're  purely in a defensive position  we need more support the furlough helps  but there does need to be other economic  support packages put in place  for all hospitality whilst we're in  these restrictions  and even then coming out of the  restrictions it isn't then going to end  there needs to be support in place the  vat help the business rates relief  helped  um that there's no doubt but they need  to continue to allow they've racked up  more debt during this period  and i worry about the next 12 months  there's going to need to be  a fair wind with us we certainly cannot  continue to keep moving in and out of  tears the way which we are else it's  just a complete devastation on people's  livelihoods and of course the greater  manchester in particular  you've been under title restrictions for  an awfully long time now  yeah i think only three weeks without  restrictions i think it's the worst hit  city  i don't think people are great to manage  have a problem with restrictions if it's  saving people's lives  they have a problem with restrictions  that aren't then um  you know where the economic package and  support isn't in place to actually align  with what's happening  you know people losing their jobs  livelihoods are being lost it's  affecting  the disadvantaged and the vulnerable  most the hospitality sector  who works in the hospitality sector who  props it up it's young people it's the  it's the lowest paid workers and they're  the people who need the income  badly and they're not getting the income  and  you know we had 250 people working in  our hotels before this pandemic  at this moment in time there isn't any  work there there isn't any work there  the pa  the the part-time staff who come in the  weekend to work on match day say for  instance around hotel football  we don't have match days so we've just  got a real big problem that's brewing  there isn't any  pathway out of it and that is the big  frustration that's why united city was  sent up we need to change the direction  of the messaging  and have a clear way out of this because  it can't continue to go on as you say  250 people straight away whose  livelihoods depend on on the future of  what happens there  so just to be clear is it the  restrictions that you've got a problem  with or is it the fact that there's not  enough economic support along with it  i don't have a problem with the  restrictions uh in respect of  we need to save lives health must be the  priority and clearly there is  high covert in manchester i think there  is high culvert in manchester in certain  aspects pockets have managed to very  high cases  and in other parts there isn't it's this  blanket approach the whole of greater  manchester which i think has been  applied to london and other areas as  well  um the restrictions i think aren't a  problem  but the lack of support and the lack of  planning to come out of it is the big  problem  the uncertainty i'll give one example  where in ter three  we're being told currently that we will  be told whether we're going into tier  two on the 16th of december  and that we can open hospitality on the  19th how can that be  we can't order the food in time you  can't open your booking engines how can  people plan you can't mobilize your  staff  so we're in a situation whereby there is  no plan everything's so  reactive and immediate and it cannot be  like that leadership can be  cannot be like that and i was critical  of the labour party earlier on in the  week because they're there to protect  the disadvantaged and the vulnerable and  the restrictions being in place which  protects health is fine  but they know that the economic support  isn't in place aligned with those  restrictions  which means you've got to take a  position and be bold and go against that  you cannot abstain  and that's where people have managed at  this moment in time i think are  frustrated with the lack of leadership  in i suppose protecting  the residents of manchester and the  communities that are hardest hit so  you'd like to see labour vote against a  tier system then  yeah i don't like half roads leadership  take us  take a stance you've got you know you go  out in your general election you're told  in your general election to all the  people on the doorsteps get out and vote  so when you're elected and you are in  that you're in that seat in west  westminster you just take a position  you don't abstain you take part in the  match you're the opposition  you're the opposition don't sit in the  stand they sat in the stand  whilst the home team had a clear run and  even they didn't have a clear run  because some of their own players got  sent out  quite a few cards in that match um  i've got to ask you what would you say  to people who say look footballers  should just stay out of politics stick  to football i'd have a problem with it  to be honest with you  the timeline is full of critics it's  full of people who support and i have no  issue with it  um i think that does everybody not feel  a little bit more political the last  couple of years  i think they do that's a good thing i  think people are more engaged maybe it's  because of the  the leaders that we've had in some of  the biggest countries in the world  they've been quite divisive or they've  engaged with people more  uh they've brought people's opinions to  the former i think brexit  i think covered the fact that we've been  at home watching government ministers  speaking every night on television  uh the fact that we have had nothing  else to do for three or four months and  other than to do that  i don't think it's a bad thing and  football players have an ability to be  able to connect  and leadership is about connecting and  you think of what football players have  done this last six months there was a  point earlier in the pandemic where i  think the health  minister suggested that footballers need  to do more  i don't think he would be saying that  today because footballers do try you  know  footballers generally are from uh  low-income families  from areas like dudley and berry and  barrow they're not from hampstead or  knightsbridge they're not from sort of  what would be wealthy families or  um you know rich families they're not  and they they  they're generally good people football  players they obviously have the faults  like everybody but they want people to  get by they want opportunities for all  they want equality they want  inclusion and to me there is too much  division  and i think that's 